(function(){'use strict';const library=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!library||!Array.isArray(library.series))return;const changes={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-24":{"number":24,"title":"A Question Worth Asking","content":"I asked Iroh who he was when nobody wanted anything from him.\n\nThe question came out while we were carrying the last dry towels to the balcony above the Jasmine Dragon. Below us, Ba Sing Se filled with evening carts and the smell of cooking oil. Someone had started singing three streets away, loudly and not particularly well.\n\nIroh looked up at the sky. \"A man who likes a quiet balcony.\"\n\n\"That's a place, not a person.\"\n\n\"A man who has brought you to a very good place.\"\n\n\"You're doing it again.\"\n\nHe took the towels from me and made a show of folding them. \"What am I doing?\"\n\n\"Answering the question that makes you sound most helpful.\"\n\nHe stopped folding.\n\nI wished, for one terrible moment, that I'd simply asked whether he wanted more tea. It would have been easier. But I wasn't falling in love with an answer to every problem. I was falling for someone who could make a joke, and make another joke, and still carry something so heavy I sometimes saw it in the way he stood up.\n\nIroh sat on the little bench. There was room beside him, but he didn't pat it. He waited.\n\n\"I used to be many things,\" he said. \"Some of them I did not choose. Others I chose badly.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I am also a man who sings off-key when he thinks no one can hear.\"\n\n\"That one's new.\"\n\n\"It is a carefully guarded secret.\"\n\nFrom the street, the unknown singer missed another note so spectacularly Iroh winced. Then he began humming the same tune and made it worse on purpose. I laughed hard enough to lean against the railing.\n\n\"You're terrible.\"\n\n\"Then I have answered at least one part of your question.\"\n\nWe sat for a while. The breeze lifted the edge of his sleeve. I noticed a tiny scorch mark from the kettle he had nearly dropped that afternoon while arguing with a supplier over the cost of dried jasmine. I touched the fabric.\n\n\"Does this happen often?\"\n\n\"Only when I am attempting to look dignified.\"\n\n\"So daily.\"\n\n\"An outrageous accusation.\"\n\nI pulled my chair closer. \"I mean it. What would you do if nobody was waiting for advice, nobody called you General or Uncle, and you could choose the afternoon for yourself?\"\n\nHe thought longer than I expected.\n\n\"I would eat something sweet that I had no intention of sharing,\" he admitted. \"I would play Pai Sho badly, so no one invited me to a serious match. I would visit a garden where nobody knew my name.\"\n\n\"Could you do any of that tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Perhaps not the garden. Mina needs me to sign the delivery order, and Lian is waiting for us to repair the hose.\"\n\n\"That's two things. Not an entire lifetime.\"\n\nHe smiled, but I could see the uncertainty beneath it.\n\n\"I've spent so much of my life being useful in order to justify the room I occupied,\" he said. \"I find it difficult to imagine occupying a room for pleasure.\"\n\nI reached for his hand, then stopped. \"Would you like me to hold it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe stayed on the balcony while a delivery cart rattled through the street below. A boy carrying buns stopped to argue with the driver about whose wheel had splashed water onto his shoes. Iroh leaned over the railing as though he might settle the matter, then drew back.\n\n\"That was very difficult,\" he admitted.\n\n\"What was?\"\n\n\"Allowing people to quarrel without offering a solution.\"\n\nI laughed. \"You can care about them without being appointed referee.\"\n\nHe sat beside me. His sleeve brushed mine, warm from the tea kettle we'd carried upstairs.\n\n\"Would you like to know something else I want?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very much.\"\n\n\"I want to be allowed to be wrong about a little thing, and not have it become evidence that I am unworthy of the life I have now.\"\n\nIt was such a quiet wish that it frightened me. I knew how many enormous mistakes stood behind his words, and how little right I had to offer absolution for harm that wasn't mine.\n\n\"I can argue with you about a burnt bun,\" I said. \"I won't use it to make a verdict on every part of your history.\"\n\nHe looked relieved, then immediately suspicious.\n\n\"You would argue about a bun?\"\n\n\"Passionately.\"\n\n\"Then you may have found your calling.\"\n\nHe stood and fetched two sweet buns from the basket we'd brought upstairs, placing them between us as though preparing for an important contest. One was slightly darker along the edge.\n\n\"Which is better?\"\n\nI took a bite of each. \"Neither. They're different.\"\n\n\"An evasion.\"\n\n\"You're the one who spent half the evening evading my questions.\"\n\nHe laughed and conceded the point. Then he asked what I wanted when nobody needed me. The question caught me by surprise.\n\n\"I want to write,\" I said. \"I want to see people I love without having to explain why loving them doesn't diminish anyone else. And sometimes I want to sit beside you without fixing anything.\"\n\nHe took my hand again. \"Then let us practice the last one.\"\n\nWe sat until the evening grew cool, sharing the buns and saying nothing important. That too was a kind of answer.\n\nHis palm was warm. It still surprised me when something that simple felt like a choice two people had made together rather than an answer one person owed.\n\n\"I'm not asking you to stop helping people,\" I said. \"But you can want things that have nothing to do with rescuing me.\"\n\n\"And what do you want?\"\n\n\"I want you to stop making me guess whether there's a person behind the wise expression.\"\n\n\"Very demanding.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHe turned toward me. \"Then I will tell you something selfish. When you leave to spend time with people you also love, I sometimes look at the upper room and wish you were in it. It is not a wish I intend to use against you.\"\n\nI swallowed. We'd spoken plainly about my ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory. Iroh had not been asked to pretend that he never missed me. I just didn't want longing to become a debt.\n\n\"You can say you miss me,\" I told him. \"You can ask whether I'm free next week. I want to hear what you need, not discover it after you've become lonely enough to be noble about it.\"\n\nHis laugh was smaller this time. \"Noble loneliness is a remarkably foolish habit.\"\n\n\"One you've perfected.\"\n\n\"I am reconsidering my achievements.\"\n\nIroh found a folded paper in the pocket of his robe and turned it over twice before giving it to me. I thought it was a letter until I saw the little squares drawn beside each name.\n\n“A rota?” I asked.\n\n“A habit,” he said. “Old men are allowed to have them.”\n\nThe names belonged to people from the White Lotus who had written offering to help at the shop. Beside each name he had marked a day, a task, and an hour. He had not asked any of them whether the hours suited.\n\n“You're recruiting an army to wash cups.”\n\n“A very small army.”\n\n“An army of people with lives.”\n\nHe looked at the squares again. “Ah.”\n\nThe balcony door rattled in the wind. He put his thumb over the top of the page, almost protective. I was beginning to see the distance between how he cared for people and how he tried to keep them safe. A roster could be an expression of love. It could also be an order disguised as generosity.\n\n“Do you want help asking them?” I said.\n\n“No.” Then he saw my expression. “I mean I would like to ask them myself. Without the comforting shield of an officer's stationery.”\n\nHe took out his brush and drew a line through the assigned hours. On the other side of the page he wrote three questions instead: *Would you like to visit? What would you enjoy doing? When would suit you?*\n\n“That is a great deal less efficient,” he said.\n\n“Terribly.”\n\n“And perhaps more likely to result in visitors who want to be here.”\n\nI leaned against him. He was warm from the kitchen, and his sleeve smelled of roasted leaves. I wondered whether a man could spend a lifetime learning the difference between being needed and being wanted. Then I decided I didn't have to answer it for him.\n\n“I'll make you a deal,” I said. “You can be wrong about the roster if I get to be wrong about the next burnt bun.”\n\n“An alarming concession. I shall consider it.”\n\nHe folded the paper again. This time the blank side faced outward.\n\n\nThe shop bell rang downstairs. Mina called that the parcel runner had delivered the repaired tea strainer and wanted a receipt. Iroh rose, then glanced at me.\n\n\"Would you mind if I took care of that?\"\n\n\"Not at all. I'll still be here.\"\n\nHe went down and came back five minutes later with a paper bag.\n\n\"Sweet buns,\" he announced. \"Bought with my own money, so the inventory will not suffer.\"\n\n\"You bought two.\"\n\n\"I have not yet learned selfishness perfectly.\"\n\nWe ate them on the bench. He got sugar on his beard. I considered telling him, then decided I liked the excuse to lean closer. I wiped it away with my thumb. He caught my wrist gently.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nWe kissed until the evening voices rose and the last light slipped between the rooftops. When we broke apart, he rested his head against my shoulder for a while.\n\n\"Call me Iroh when we're alone,\" he said.\n\n\"I already do.\"\n\n\"Then keep doing it. I like the way you say it.\"\n\nBelow, the city went on needing things. Above it, the man beside me wanted another sweet bun and someone to laugh with. That was more than enough for tonight.","word_count":1656},"avatar-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Things Iroh Cannot Carry","content":"The bed shook once beneath me.\n\nNot from an earthquake. Iroh had sat upright so quickly that the mattress shifted against the wall. The room above the Jasmine Dragon was dark, and his hand had closed around the blanket until the cloth twisted like a rope.\n\n\"Iroh?\"\n\nHe did not answer.\n\nRain tapped the roof tiles. Down below, a cart rolled past the shuttered shop, its wheels catching on the same loose paving stone I had been meaning to report. An ordinary night in Ba Sing Se. I said the city aloud, slowly.\n\n\"We're upstairs. It's raining. There's a cart outside.\"\n\nHis breathing changed.\n\nHe looked toward the door.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\n\"Here.\"\n\n\"Forgive me. I must have disturbed you.\"\n\n\"You did wake me.\" I pulled the blanket back over my knees. \"That's different from asking for forgiveness.\"\n\nI could see his expression only when the street lantern caught the side of his face. He looked tired in a way that didn't belong to one bad dream.\n\n\"Would you like a light?\" I asked.\n\n\"A small one.\"\n\nI lit the lamp near the window. I didn't touch him, though I wanted to. His fingers were still curled against the blanket.\n\n\"Do you want me close?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not suddenly.\"\n\nI moved beside him and rested my hand open between us. After a moment he took it.\n\nThe quiet stretched. He wasn't going to tell me what he'd dreamed, and I didn't want to turn the silence into an interrogation.\n\nThen he said, \"In the dream, I knew where the men were going. I kept sending them anyway.\"\n\nI thought of the siege he rarely described and the grief that had followed him long after the fighting stopped.\n\n\"You don't have to finish that sentence.\"\n\n\"I know. I wish knowing it made the memory less loud.\"\n\nHe swallowed.\n\n\"I am very good at telling other people to rest. I am considerably less talented at obeying myself.\"\n\n\"That sounds familiar.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You?\"\n\n\"I can organize a whole square after a flood, then forget to eat until someone points at a bowl.\"\n\n\"An appalling habit.\"\n\n\"Yours or mine?\"\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nHe laughed once, without much joy.\n\nI asked whether he wanted tea. He said no; he didn't want to go downstairs and start treating the night like another shift. We stayed where we were until his hand relaxed.\n\n\"Would it help to speak to someone about the nightmares?\" I asked eventually.\n\nHe looked toward the lamp.\n\n\"Someone outside this room?\"\n\n\"Someone you choose. It doesn't have to be me.\"\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n\"Are you tired of listening?\"\n\nI was hurt by the question, then felt ashamed of being hurt.\n\n\"No. I'm saying I can love you and still not know how to help with every part of what you carry. I don't want my being here to become the only thing between you and a night like this.\"\n\nHe considered that. There was no comfortable way to tell someone I loved that I wasn't a cure, but I wanted him to believe I wasn't leaving because I said it.\n\n\"I know a healer who works with veterans,\" he said finally. \"She has written to me before.\"\n\n\"Would you like to write back?\"\n\n\"Perhaps in the morning.\"\n\n\"Then in the morning.\"\n\nHe set the lamp lower. I rested against him only after he moved his arm to make room. We did not go back to sleep quickly.\n\nAt dawn, Mina knocked on the downstairs door. Someone had arrived early with a delivery and needed the dry-goods store opened.\n\nIroh started to rise.\n\n\"I can get it,\" I said. \"You're allowed to stay upstairs.\"\n\n\"Are you certain?\"\n\n\"I can open a door and sign a receipt. I've had extensive training.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nMina and I checked the jars, rejected one cracked lid, and recorded the shortage. She didn't ask about our night. I didn't offer it.\n\nWhen I returned with breakfast, Iroh had placed a clean sheet of paper beside his cup.\n\n\"I have written a letter,\" he said.\n\n\"To the healer?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"I have not told her the dream. Only that I would like an appointment and that I prefer to speak privately.\"\n\n\"That sounds like enough to begin.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\nWe took the letter to the post, then walked slowly along the sheltered market street.\n\nThe post clerk glanced at the address and told Iroh that a courier could collect the reply directly from the shop next time. He thanked her, then asked whether that meant the letter would arrive sooner. She explained the schedules and, with admirable patience, corrected his assumption about the mountain route.\n\nOutside, a man pushing a handcart struggled to get past a pile of empty crates. Iroh reached forward as though he might lift the entire thing himself.\n\n\"Should we ask?\" I said.\n\nHe nodded and approached.\n\n\"Would another pair of hands help?\"\n\nThe man shook his head. \"I need the shopkeeper to move the crates. If you lift the cart, it'll only block the lane again.\"\n\nIroh looked at the crates, then at me.\n\n\"You are enjoying this,\" he said.\n\n\"Immensely.\"\n\nWe found the shopkeeper and explained the obstruction without making it a crime. The crates were moved. The cart passed on its own wheels.\n\nThere were days when helping meant doing less than the first heroic impulse suggested.\n\nIroh watched the man disappear around the corner.\n\n\"That has become a theme in my life.\"\n\n\"I think it's a good one.\"\n\nHe turned his face toward the breeze and allowed himself a small, tired smile.\n\nI knew the nightmares weren't a problem a clear street could solve. But the morning had room for a difficult letter, a useful question, and the choice not to take charge of another person's cart. Rain had washed the stones dark. Iroh offered his arm, and I took it.\n\n\"I hope you do not think my weakness changes how I feel about you,\" he said.\n\n\"I don't think fear makes you weak.\"\n\n\"You have said that before.\"\n\n\"And you've listened before. We're both allowed to need another reminder.\"\n\nAt the corner, the baker was opening her shutters. She waved. Iroh waved back, a little shyly.\n\nWe had told each other about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory long before that morning. He knew I would cross to Lanternweald again, and that I loved other people. None of that changed how seriously I took what he'd trusted me with. I didn't promise to be the only person he could ask for help. I promised to remain truthful about when I could be here.\n\nBack in the courtyard, he set out two cups. The first tea was too weak.\n\n\"Terrible,\" I said.\n\n\"An offense against civilization.\"\n\n\"Shall we try again?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe made a second pot.\n\nIn the afternoon, a delivery boy arrived with a torn dispatch pouch and a list of streets he had been told to avoid. Somebody had circulated a rumor that the old war tunnels beneath the district were reopening. He wanted Iroh to tell him whether the rumor was true.\n\nThe look that passed over Iroh's face made my stomach tighten. For a second he was back in a command tent, sorting routes before anyone else could say they were afraid.\n\n“I cannot tell you about tunnels I haven't inspected,” he said.\n\n“But you know the city.”\n\n“I know a great many versions of the city.”\n\nThe boy twisted the strap of his pouch. “Then who do I ask?”\n\nIroh took out a sheet of shop paper and wrote down the district works office, the posted road closures, and the public notice board. He did not draw a secret route or offer to become the keeper of everybody's movements.\n\n“If they cannot answer, come back,” he said. “Not so I can promise safety. So I can help you ask again.”\n\nThe boy left with the paper tucked inside his coat.\n\n“That cost you,” I said after the bell stopped ringing.\n\nIroh lowered himself into the chair beside the stove. “I would have preferred to know everything.”\n\n“Me too.”\n\n“The dream made it difficult to remember that knowledge has boundaries.”\n\nI crouched to pick up the dispatch strap he'd dropped. I didn't touch his shoulder until he nodded. The kettle was cold, and the kitchen smelled faintly of yesterday's citrus peel.\n\nHe asked Mina to cover the counter for ten minutes. She checked the schedule, agreed to five, and brought him a cup of hot water without asking why he needed it. He thanked her and counted the minutes himself.\n\nThere was no victory in that afternoon. There was a rumor, a boy trying to get home, and a man who had chosen not to disguise uncertainty as authority. When Iroh stood up again, he took the empty cup back to the sink.\n\n\nThat evening he asked me to sit beside him while he read the healer's reply. I did. It offered a private appointment the following week. He wrote the date in his own calendar instead of asking me to manage it for him.\n\nLater he kissed me softly, his fingers still warm from the cup. I kissed him back, and we closed the bedroom door on a night that belonged to us, not the war.\n\nThe nightmares had not vanished. But there was another name in the notebook, another kind of help, and a morning waiting after the dark.","word_count":1574},"avatar-34":{"number":34,"title":"Under the Same Lantern","content":"The lantern seller wanted to charge Iroh twice as much because he recognized him.\n\nI knew the moment it happened. The seller's shoulders went stiff, and his gaze moved from the old man's face to the fine embroidery at his collar.\n\n\"For the Dragon of the West,\" he said, \"this one would be appropriate.\"\n\nHe lifted a lantern with enormous painted flames. It was beautiful in the way an expensive decoration could be beautiful when someone else was paying for it. Iroh studied it with grave interest.\n\n\"I was considering the little turtleduck,\" he said.\n\nThe seller looked baffled. The turtleduck lantern was crooked, inexpensive, and painted with one eye slightly larger than the other.\n\nI loved it immediately.\n\n\"That one,\" I said.\n\n\"Of course,\" the seller replied, recovering. \"For your companion.\"\n\nThe word made me hesitate. It wasn't wrong, but the market was crowded. People knew Iroh; some of them also knew enough about his history to be curious. I had no intention of turning an evening's walk into a public explanation of who loved whom.\n\nIroh noticed my pause.\n\n\"Would you prefer we look elsewhere?\" he asked.\n\n\"No. I want the turtleduck.\"\n\n\"Then we shall have the turtleduck.\"\n\nHe paid the marked price and accepted change, despite the seller's attempt to refuse it. I carried the lantern while he carried a paper bag of steamed buns. We had promised Mina we would not return to the shop with a pile of unbudgeted decorations, so we were already operating at the very edge of our allowance.\n\n\"She will be merciless,\" I said.\n\n\"She has that right.\"\n\n\"She'll say you bought an animal that can't swim.\"\n\n\"It is a lantern. Its swimming abilities are not relevant.\"\n\nThe market curved along the river, where strings of smaller lights reflected in black water. Children ran between stalls. A pair of musicians played an uneven tune near the bridge. A woman selling ribbons called out that she had one shade of gold left before closing.\n\nIroh offered me his arm.\n\nI wanted it. I also wanted the option to let go if the crowd became too much.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said, slipping my hand around his elbow. \"But if someone starts asking personal questions, I get to stop the conversation.\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\n\"And you don't have to introduce me as anything you're uncomfortable saying.\"\n\nHe stopped walking.\n\n\"I am not ashamed of you.\"\n\n\"I didn't say you were.\"\n\n\"No.\" He considered. \"You are asking me not to turn my pride into an announcement you did not choose.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nA child nearly collided with my knees while chasing a loose lantern. Iroh caught the lantern by its handle, handed it back, and asked the child to walk near the water. He didn't offer a speech about rivers. The child ran off anyway. We shared a look.\n\n\"An excellent success,\" he said.\n\n\"Your authority is terrifying.\"\n\nWe passed a familiar voice. Ren stood at a stall buying stationery, her arms full of papers. She greeted Iroh with a discreet nod, then looked at me.\n\n\"Enjoying the festival?\"\n\n\"I am,\" I said. \"Especially because he hasn't tried to buy the whole street.\"\n\n\"Give him time.\"\n\nIroh sighed. \"I am surrounded by critics.\"\n\nRen left with a smile. She didn't ask about our plans or the relationships I'd spoken about privately with Iroh. Not every person who knew him needed to know everything about us.\n\nA small group had gathered beside the musicians, debating whether to hang a row of lanterns across the narrow market passage. The seller who owned the next stall objected; the string would catch the awning every time he lowered it.\n\nIroh looked at the empty space above the crowd.\n\n\"Perhaps if we tie it higher,\" he began.\n\n\"Then the ladder will block the street,\" I said.\n\nThe seller nodded, grateful someone had noticed.\n\nWe walked around the stalls until we found a wider arch where the musicians could still see the display. The lantern maker offered to lend a safer string, but only if somebody agreed to collect it before the market closed. Ren took the evening shift after checking her own plans.\n\n\"An evening of extraordinary negotiations,\" Iroh murmured.\n\n\"All so a turtleduck can glow.\"\n\nHe lifted the crooked lantern. \"A very noble turtleduck.\"\n\nThe children passing beneath the arch laughed at its uneven eye. One asked whether she could carry it in the procession. Iroh looked at me.\n\n\"Does she want to?\" I asked her.\n\n\"Only to the bridge.\"\n\n\"Then ask the lantern keeper whether it's allowed.\"\n\nIt was. The child carried the lantern a short distance, handed it back carefully, and ran to catch up with her family. We walked behind her, with our hands almost touching.\n\nIroh glanced at me. \"Are you worried about being seen together?\"\n\n\"Not with you. I'm worried about strangers deciding what being seen means they can ask.\"\n\n\"Then we need not answer.\"\n\nI smiled. Our relationship had room for public tenderness and private choices. I'd told him openly that I preferred polyamory and was ethically non-monogamous, and he hadn't treated that truth as a riddle for the market to solve.\n\nWhen the slower music began, he offered his hand again. I took it. There were no speeches, only his ridiculous lantern, a tune we could barely follow, and the faint warmth of his fingers around mine.\n\nFarther along, the musicians started a slower song. Iroh offered his hand. \"Would you dance?\"\n\n\"Here?\"\n\n\"Unless you have another river in mind.\"\n\n\"I'm terrible.\"\n\n\"Then we will be terrible together.\"\n\nWe danced beside the railing. It was more of a careful shuffle than anything that could be called elegant, and I laughed when he misjudged a step and nearly turned the turtleduck upside down. He caught my hand before I could trip. I caught his sleeve. We steadied each other, still laughing.\n\nFor a moment I forgot we were being watched at all. Then I noticed a couple smiling from a nearby bench. I felt the old instinct to explain myself, to describe every other relationship so nobody would assume this was the only person in my life.\n\nI didn't owe them that. Neither did Iroh.\n\n\"I like being here with you,\" I said.\n\nHe rested his forehead briefly against mine. \"And I with you.\"\n\n\"What if people misunderstand?\"\n\n\"Then we can correct what matters, and let the rest pass.\"\n\n\"That sounds dangerously reasonable.\"\n\n\"I am occasionally afflicted.\"\n\nHe asked before kissing me. I answered by taking one small step closer. The kiss tasted of sweet bun and cold evening air. A nearby musician missed a note; Iroh laughed against my cheek, and that made me laugh too.\n\nWe walked home beneath the lanterns without pretending our public affection had resolved every private agreement. I was still ethically non-monogamous, still happiest with polyamory, and still free to make plans with other people who knew and chose that arrangement. Iroh knew, and he had the same freedom to name his own needs.\n\nThe walk back took us through the narrow row of candle stalls. An older vendor was extinguishing his lanterns one by one, careful to save the short wicks for the following night. As we passed, his eyes lingered on Iroh and then on our joined hands.\n\n“General,” he said softly, “my brother served under you.”\n\nI felt Iroh's fingers loosen. He didn't pull away. “I hope your brother came home.”\n\n“He did.” The vendor took down another lantern. “He never spoke about that winter. Not until the day he died.”\n\nI didn't know whether to stand still or keep walking. Iroh's face had become very quiet.\n\n“I am sorry for what he carried,” he said.\n\nThe man looked at our turtleduck lantern. Its crooked eye threw a ridiculous shadow across the stones.\n\n“People say you changed,” the vendor continued. “I suppose that's something.”\n\n“It is not a receipt for what happened before.”\n\nThe vendor gave one short nod. Neither of them asked me to smooth over the silence. We bought a fresh wick at the ordinary posted price, and the man wrapped it in a scrap of paper without offering further judgment.\n\nAround the corner Iroh stopped. He looked down at our hands.\n\n“Would you prefer I let go when someone recognizes me?” he asked.\n\n“No. But you can tell me when you need a minute.”\n\nHe laughed without much humor. “I seem to need several.”\n\n“Then we can take several.”\n\nWe stood beneath the awning while a little rain returned. I touched the damp edge of his cuff. He lifted it and kissed my fingers once, with none of the polished amusement he'd used at the lantern stall. I loved that the gesture was awkward. It meant he hadn't prepared it for an audience.\n\nWhen we moved again, he held my hand as lightly as before. We carried the wick and the crooked turtleduck home instead of trying to carry the vendor's whole history.\n\n\nAt the shop, Mina saw the turtleduck and put both hands on her hips.\n\n\"How much?\"\n\n\"The price on the tag,\" I said.\n\nIroh looked proud of himself.\n\n\"Good,\" Mina said. \"Then tomorrow you can help me fix its crooked eye.\"\n\nWe hung it above the long table, where it cast a small, uneven pool of light. Later he invited me upstairs. I said yes, and we left the door closed on the part of the evening that didn't belong to the market.\n\nBy morning the turtleduck had become Mina's favorite lantern. She would never admit it, but she moved it away from the stove so the heat couldn't scorch the paint. I saw Iroh notice. Neither of us said a word.","word_count":1610},"avatar-40":{"number":40,"title":"Jasmine After Rain","content":"After the rain, the Jasmine Dragon smelled of wet brick and orange peel.\n\nI found Iroh standing beside the window, watching water slide down the outside of the glass. The street below was crowded with people stepping around puddles. A cart wheel struck a loose paving stone and sent a little spray over its driver's boots.\n\nIroh winced in sympathy.\n\n\"That stone needs to be reset,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes. And if I go outside to reset it now, Mina will remind me the shop's roof needs attention first.\"\n\n\"She's right.\"\n\n\"An alarming trend.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nWe had spent the past week repairing small things: a leaking gutter, an uneven shelf, a stubborn back door that swelled whenever it rained. The projects weren't glorious. There were receipts, delays and arguments about whether the money could stretch to one more patch before the season changed.\n\nIroh had treated each task with the same serious care he once might have given a diplomatic mission. He was learning to say when he was tired; I was learning not to hear every unfinished job as an accusation.\n\nThe shop had only just reopened after the rain.\n\nMina came from the kitchen with a handwritten list.\n\n\"The supplier delivered enough ginger for two days, not three,\" she said. \"If we serve the special all weekend, we'll run out before the next cart.\"\n\nIroh studied the sheet.\n\n\"Then we shall limit the special.\"\n\n\"Please tell the customers,\" Mina said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nShe looked between us.\n\n\"And please don't spend the afternoon arguing about whose turn it is to climb onto the roof.\"\n\nIroh's mouth twitched.\n\n\"Your faith in my judgment is touching.\"\n\n\"I trust your judgment. I don't trust that ladder.\"\n\nShe returned to the kitchen.\n\nI went to the notice board and wrote the updated menu. The change was boring, practical and necessary. We had enough ingredients for the house blend, and the regular customers could still come in from the wet street to drink something warm.\n\nThe first person through the door was Halen.\n\nHe stopped beneath the awning, shaking rain from his sleeves.\n\n\"Did I miss the special?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" I said. \"There are a few servings left today.\"\n\nHe nodded, then pointed at the window table.\n\n\"Is that one free?\"\n\nI looked to Iroh. Halen had been coming less often since his sister died. We didn't have the right to turn every visit into a conversation about grief.\n\n\"It's free,\" Iroh said. \"Would you like it?\"\n\n\"Yes, please.\"\n\nHe sat. We served his tea, then left him to look out at the rain.\n\nIroh watched the street for another moment.\n\n\"Do you ever feel that the shop asks too much of us?\" he asked quietly.\n\n\"Sometimes. Not always.\"\n\nHe looked at the door.\n\n\"I spent so many years moving away from places where people needed me to be a general. I thought a small shop would make it simple to stay.\"\n\n\"Has it?\"\n\n\"No.\" He smiled faintly. \"I have discovered that people can need a great many things from a man who only meant to sell tea.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to decide what you can offer.\"\n\n\"I know you keep telling me.\"\n\n\"I'm going to keep telling you until you believe it.\"\n\nHe took my hand, then seemed to think better of it because Mina was calling for help with the dry-goods shelf. He looked torn between the room and the kitchen.\n\n\"Go help her,\" I said. \"I'll watch the counter.\"\n\n\"Would you mind?\"\n\n\"No. But come back after. We need to talk about next week.\"\n\nHis expression changed.\n\n\"Is something wrong?\"\n\n\"Not wrong. A plan.\"\n\nHe nodded and went to the back.\n\nI served two customers and counted the remaining portions of ginger before lowering the special's availability on the menu. The afternoon passed in the comforting rhythm of a business that could run without becoming a shrine to anyone's past.\n\nWhen Iroh returned, he carried a short piece of wood.\n\n\"What's that?\" I asked.\n\n\"The shelf support. It was less heroic than it appeared.\"\n\n\"Did you fix it?\"\n\n\"Mina did. I held the shelf.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nWe closed early when the street flooded again near the market corner. Nobody was trapped; the posted upper route remained open. The safer decision was to clean, bring the outdoor sign inside and let the rain pass.\n\nIroh made two cups of tea. We sat at the table where we had first argued about whether happiness was another thing a person had to deserve.\n\n\"I'll be crossing to Lanternweald next week,\" I said. \"There is a scheduled meeting about the shared drainage. Afterward I have time with Maui, and a visit to Moonroot.\"\n\nHe looked at his cup.\n\n\"Thank you for telling me.\"\n\n\"I wanted to ask about our next date before I put anything on the calendar.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Thursday evening. I can close at six if Mina wishes to take the late shift.\"\n\n\"Only if she agrees. And only if she gets the hours she actually wants.\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI studied him.\n\n\"Are you worried?\"\n\nHe laughed without much humor.\n\n\"I seem to have spent most of my life expecting a good thing to be taken away.\"\n\n\"Do you think me loving other people means I'm going to stop loving you?\"\n\n\"No.\" He met my eyes. \"But there are days when knowing something and feeling it are not the same.\"\n\nI took a slow breath.\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I told you that before we began, and I meant it. I also mean it when I say I want to come back here. I don't want to promise I'm never going to miss a train or have a difficult conversation. I want us to be able to say when it hurts.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"And I must not turn the hurt into a command.\"\n\n\"Neither of us should.\"\n\nHe looked out at the wet street.\n\n\"Perhaps this is what frightened me most. Happiness used to feel like a debt. If I enjoyed it, I believed something would be expected of me in return.\"\n\n\"What would you like it to be now?\"\n\nHe took my hand across the table.\n\n\"A cup of tea. A date we agree to. The freedom to tell you when I am afraid.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That sounds manageable.\"\n\n\"Not always.\"\n\n\"No. But real.\"\n\nHe leaned toward me.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was gentle at first. When I reached up to touch his cheek, he smiled against my mouth. The kettle began to whistle, as if it objected to being ignored.\n\nI laughed.\n\nIroh sighed.\n\n\"Even my kitchen insists upon participating.\"\n\nWe turned off the stove, put away the cups and closed the shutters. Upstairs, the room smelled faintly of cedar. We left the rest of the evening unwritten, ours to keep.\n\nBefore going upstairs, we heard a knock at the service door. It was Bo from Jasmine Steps, carrying a clipboard speckled with rain and a folded notice from the district works office.\n\n“The lane behind your shop is going to close tomorrow,” Bo said. “They found a cracked drain collar by the tea deliveries.”\n\nIroh took the notice. “The closure is sensible.”\n\n“Good. Because two vendors have already asked me to ignore it for the morning rush.”\n\nHe almost smiled. “A familiar temptation.”\n\nBo spread the map across the counter. The alternate delivery route passed a narrow ramp used by the clinic and the upstairs residents. Heavy carts would make it impassable.\n\n“Could we move the cart times?” I asked.\n\n“Only if the drivers agree and somebody pays them for the early start,” Bo replied. “The works office isn't doing that.”\n\nIroh reached for the ledger. I saw the old instinct assemble a plan in his expression, but this time he started with a question.\n\n“Who should be at the discussion?”\n\n“Drivers. Clinic. Mina. The vendors who'll lose the morning slot. Then us.”\n\nWe wrote their names on separate slips instead of putting them into a timetable. Bo agreed to deliver invitations, not assignments. Mina, who had been listening from the kitchen, insisted that any new delivery hour appear on the paid rota. Iroh opened the cash drawer, checked the sum, and said that the special could wait until the road was safe.\n\nWhen Bo left, I stood near the kettle with a slip of paper between my fingers. It said *ask before deciding*. The ink had spread slightly where I'd touched it.\n\n“Somewhat less romantic than a lantern festival,” I said.\n\nIroh came close enough for me to feel the warmth of his shoulder. “A safe kitchen has its charms.”\n\n“That's a dreadful line.”\n\n“It was never intended for publication.”\n\nI kissed him before he could improve it. He tasted of the last cup of tea, and for once the interruption at the door had left us with work we could share rather than a crisis he had to solve alone.\n\n\nIn the morning the clouds had opened. Light shone on the wet street and the old paving stone.\n\nMina arrived with a carpenter's estimate for the roof.\n\n\"Before you offer to climb anything,\" she said, \"read this.\"\n\nIroh did.\n\nThen he reached for the shop ledger and began to work out how to pay for it.\n\nThe rain had ended. The work hadn't. Neither had our life together.","word_count":1554},"avatar-42":{"number":42,"title":"Six Tables and One Teapot","content":"The trouble with the Six Tables feast was that there were only five usable tables.\n\nThe sixth had a loose leg.\n\nMara discovered the problem while placing bread on it. One basket leaned. The other followed. She caught both, but not before declaring the furniture an insult to bakers everywhere.\n\n\"I made that table,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Then you know who to complain to.\"\n\nIroh set down his teapot.\n\n\"Perhaps we could put it near the wall and reserve it for cups.\"\n\nMara pointed at the wobble.\n\n\"Do you want the tea in the cups or on the floor?\"\n\nI rubbed the bridge of my nose.\n\nThe feast was supposed to be simple. A community meal on Lanternwell Square, free to attend, with food brought by anyone who wished and enough seating for people who needed it. Greg had agreed to play music. Katara had arranged for two clinic volunteers to help serve. Neri had promised not to count how many guests arrived because the new privacy protocol made that an increasingly unpopular habit.\n\nThen everyone arrived at once.\n\nAang came through the Riverstone Door carrying three boxes of flatbread. Toph followed, announcing that the floor near the eastern arch was \"one good sneeze from embarrassment.\" Danse appeared with tools because somebody had mentioned a table. Greg brought two folding chairs and a guitar.\n\nMaui brought a banner.\n\nThe banner read SIX TABLES, ONE HEART.\n\nI stared at it.\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"One heart?\"\n\n\"It's a metaphor.\"\n\n\"It's a terrible metaphor.\"\n\nHe looked wounded. \"It's festive.\"\n\n\"Some of us would like more than one heart involved in a community dinner.\"\n\nHe glanced toward Iroh, then at Greg, and finally understood why I was trying so hard not to laugh.\n\n\"Fine,\" he said. \"Six tables. Many hearts.\"\n\n\"Better.\"\n\nHe went to find fresh paint.\n\nIroh watched the exchange over the rim of his cup.\n\n\"Your patience is being tested.\"\n\n\"My patience left at breakfast.\"\n\n\"Then we will have to make tea without it.\"\n\nHe had agreed to serve one modest pot of the better leaves for the feast, while the pantry volunteers handled the larger kettles. It was a good plan. We had even written it down.\n\nThen a guest arrived carrying a jug of milk and asked whether anyone had considered a child with a dairy allergy.\n\nKatara was already checking the labels.\n\nGreg set his guitar aside and helped move the milk to a separate table. Toph tapped a foot against the stone and announced that the sixth table was not the only thing wobbling.\n\nBy noon, the square had become a collection of small problems, none catastrophic, all capable of making someone feel unwelcome.\n\nI found Pip near the fountain with a stack of place cards.\n\n\"Why are there names on the chairs?\" I asked.\n\n\"People thought it would be easier to seat everyone by the person they came with.\"\n\nI picked up a card bearing my name and saw three more cards beside it.\n\nMy stomach sank.\n\n\"Who wrote these?\"\n\n\"Someone from the welcome committee.\"\n\n\"Did they ask any of us?\"\n\nPip shook their head.\n\nI put the cards back in the stack.\n\n\"Then we won't use them.\"\n\nIt would have been easy to laugh. People were fascinated by who sat next to whom, as though a dinner arrangement could explain all the choices that made a relationship honest. But I had promised the people I cared about that our private agreements wouldn't become decorations for a public event.\n\nWe removed the cards.\n\nA few people complained. Iroh explained that the tables belonged to everyone, not to whichever gossip had reached the square first.\n\n\"Guests may sit wherever they are comfortable,\" he said. \"Or stand. Or eat at home. Hospitality is not an examination.\"\n\nI wanted to kiss him for that. Instead, I carried a tray.\n\nThe feast began late.\n\nGreg played a song about repairing chairs that made Tamsin threaten to throw a hammer into the canal. Katara directed the clinic volunteers with the focused patience of someone who had triaged considerably worse situations. Aang talked with two children about the difference between games and traditions that deserved context.\n\nMaui returned with a corrected banner.\n\nSIX TABLES, MANY HEARTS, PLEASE DO NOT SIT ON THE BROKEN ONE.\n\nIt was perfect.\n\nI almost told him so.\n\nThen I noticed the guest at the far edge of the square.\n\nThey were sitting on a low wall with an untouched bowl, their shoulders hunched. People passed in front of them without looking directly at their face. It was possible they wanted to be left alone. It was also possible they had nowhere to sit.\n\nI took a spare chair from the stack and stopped a few feet away.\n\n\"Would you like a chair?\" I asked.\n\n\"No, thank you.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nI turned away.\n\n\"Wait,\" they said.\n\nI looked back.\n\n\"Does the meal cost anything?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Yes. People contribute if they can. People eat if they're hungry.\"\n\nThey studied the bowl in their hands.\n\n\"Someone told me I could help wash dishes.\"\n\n\"You can. But you don't have to earn your food.\"\n\nTheir face tightened in a way I recognized. Sometimes people had been given so much conditional kindness that an uncomplicated offer felt like a trap.\n\n\"I don't want to be counted,\" they said.\n\n\"Then we won't count you.\"\n\nI set the chair nearby, not close enough to imply they had accepted it. Then I walked back to the others.\n\nWhen I returned later with fresh water, they were sitting in it.\n\nIroh noticed the change.\n\nHe came beside me as the first lanterns lit above us.\n\n\"You did not try to persuade them.\"\n\n\"They said no.\"\n\n\"And then they found their way to yes.\"\n\n\"Maybe. Or maybe they just wanted a chair.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nAcross the square, Greg was teaching Maui a chorus. Maui sang far too loudly. Katara laughed when Toph demanded a better rhythm. Danse and Tamsin had finally repaired the sixth table, and Mara was inspecting it with the suspicion of someone who had been betrayed by furniture before.\n\nBy the time the bread ran low, people had begun volunteering things the feast didn't need. A visiting merchant offered a brass serving bell in exchange for having his name announced. Maui considered giving a speech. Greg considered giving the bell a drum solo. Mara considered locking the pantry.\n\n“Food first,” she said. “Then furniture. Then anybody who wants a solo can perform it outside.”\n\nGreg held up both hands. “I was mostly kidding.”\n\n“Mostly is doing a lot of work,” I told him.\n\nIroh caught my eye. “We may have a problem with the number of people asking for seconds.”\n\n“Do we have enough?”\n\n“We have enough for the children and the workers who haven't eaten yet. After that, no.”\n\nHe didn't make the shortage disappear. He asked Mara which portions she had reserved for volunteers, then checked the counts with her instead of inventing a more generous number. When a visitor complained, Iroh explained the limit in the same patient voice he'd used to greet him. There was an edge beneath the courtesy that reminded me of a commander holding a line.\n\nGreg set the guitar aside to help carry water, and Maui was given the very unglamorous task of folding chairs. He tried to bow while holding three at once. Toph told him that if he dropped one, he would have to listen to her describe the sound for a week.\n\nDanse inspected the fixed table with Tamsin and signed the repair sheet. Their signatures mattered more than the banner. Six places at a table weren't six invitations if somebody could lose their seat to a loose screw.\n\nMara's last basket went to the kitchen volunteers. A couple from the far district brought their own food and chose to eat near the open arch instead of joining the circle. No one chased them with an invitation.\n\nI was glad the feast wasn't perfect. The ledger would have a real total, the music would end when people were tired, and we would all have to return the borrowed chairs. That felt like a celebration inhabited by people rather than a painting of one.\n\n\nIroh reached for my hand beneath the edge of the serving cloth.\n\n\"You have been running all day,\" he said.\n\n\"So have you.\"\n\n\"Perhaps we should set a poor example and rest.\"\n\n\"I could be convinced.\"\n\nHe poured the last two cups from the special pot. The tea was faintly sweet. I took mine and sat beside him on the steps, watching the celebration continue without us.\n\nA gentle breeze lifted the hem of my sleeve. Iroh brushed a crumb from the edge of my mouth with his thumb, then waited.\n\n\"You have an audience,\" I murmured.\n\n\"Then they will have to practice looking elsewhere.\"\n\nI leaned into his kiss. It was warm and soft and lasted long enough for the music to become background noise.\n\nWhen we parted, I heard a small cough.\n\nMara stood behind us with a tray.\n\n\"If you two are finished being disgustingly sweet, somebody has to put the cups away.\"\n\nIroh accepted the tray with perfect dignity.\n\n\"I am told love involves service.\"\n\n\"Love can carry the dishes,\" Mara said.\n\nSo we did.\n\nBy the end of the evening, all six tables were occupied, nobody had been forced into a seat, and the quiet guest had gone home with a loaf of bread they hadn't needed to earn.\n\nFor a festival advertised with so much optimism, that seemed like a reasonable success.","word_count":1602}},"love-and-duty":{"love-and-duty-44":{"number":44,"title":"The City from Above","content":"Appa did not approve of my map.\n\nHe made that clear by sneezing directly onto it.\n\nThe ink smudged across the east bank. I yelped, Aang laughed, and the enormous sky bison looked entirely satisfied with his contribution to urban planning.\n\n\"It's waterproof ink,\" Aang said.\n\n\"Apparently not bison-proof.\"\n\n\"Very little is.\"\n\nI held the paper away from Appa's nose. The purpose of the flight was simple: survey the Riverstone floodplain from above, compare it with the older drainage diagram, and determine whether the recent bridge repairs had changed the way stormwater traveled. We had been asked to make observations. No one had asked us to solve an entire watershed before breakfast.\n\nAang had somehow brought enough food for a journey across three continents.\n\n\"Katara packed most of it before she left,\" he admitted as he adjusted the saddle.\n\nThat stopped me.\n\n\"She packed this?\"\n\n\"She said I'd forget to eat while I was working.\"\n\n\"Accurate.\"\n\nHe smiled, but it didn't last.\n\nWe'd received one letter from the South. Katara was helping establish treatment rooms after a difficult storm season, and none of us knew when she'd be finished. The calendar we'd made before her departure was still on the kitchen wall, her planned evenings penciled in rather than crossed out.\n\nI didn't want her absence turned into an emergency. I did want to hear her voice.\n\n\"Ready?\" Aang asked.\n\nI tucked the map into its case.\n\n\"Let's go.\"\n\nAppa rose over Lanternwell Square with a slow, powerful motion that pulled my stomach toward my boots. The rooftops shrank beneath us. The seven districts took shape as actual places instead of a list of names: Tideglass Cove's bright water, Jasmine Steps curving around the garden, Brasswatch Yard with its straight workshop paths, Moonroot's dark roof, and the thin line of Starwake Pier reaching toward its own doorway.\n\nFrom above, the Lanternweald looked small enough to cup in two hands.\n\nIt was not small enough to manage carelessly.\n\n\"Look at that,\" Aang said.\n\nHe pointed toward the spring below the well. Water had gathered behind a raised strip of earth, then diverted toward the gardens. A newer footpath crossed the old streambed at an angle.\n\n\"That's not on Fen's plan,\" I said.\n\n\"Or Neri's.\"\n\nWe landed in a clear meadow above Riverstone Terrace. I drew what we'd seen while Aang paced the path and tested the soil with gentle gusts of air. His bending could clear leaves from a blocked channel. It could reveal which stones were loose. It could not make a badly placed building disappear without deciding what would happen to the people inside it.\n\n\"Could you bend the water away?\" I asked.\n\n\"Temporarily.\"\n\n\"And when you stop?\"\n\n\"It finds its old route.\"\n\nWe stood in the grass for a long time.\n\nBelow us, a small market garden had been built on what appeared to be the natural spillway. The beds were productive. Someone had planted beans, herbs, and a row of purple flowers along the edges. Moving it would cost labor, food, and perhaps someone's only income.\n\nAang crouched beside the soil.\n\n\"I used to think if I could move the water, I'd fixed the problem,\" he said.\n\n\"Sometimes you have.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I've moved it into somebody else's home.\"\n\nI marked the garden on the map.\n\n\"Then we ask the people who live there.\"\n\nWe followed the slope down to a house with blue shutters. A woman named Sela met us at the gate, wiping dirt from her hands. She had grown up tending the garden, and her grandmother had planted its first fruit tree.\n\n\"Floodwater used to run through here,\" she told us, \"but the old stones kept it low. After the new path went in, it started collecting against the west wall.\"\n\nAang looked toward the path.\n\n\"Who approved it?\"\n\n\"The neighborhood council. They needed a wider route for carts.\"\n\nI felt a familiar irritation settle behind my ribs. Every improvement carried a cost; too often the cost was paid by someone who hadn't been at the meeting.\n\n\"Did they ask you?\" I said.\n\n\"They showed us the plan after the work began.\"\n\nAang's expression changed.\n\nHe had helped design more than one public road. He knew what it felt like to be certain a plan was generous and discover that certainty had hidden an oversight.\n\n\"Would you be willing to meet with the survey group?\" he asked.\n\nSela looked at him.\n\n\"Only if they're prepared to listen.\"\n\n\"I'll make sure they are.\"\n\n\"Don't make promises for other people,\" I murmured.\n\nAang caught himself.\n\n\"I'll ask them to listen,\" he corrected. \"And I'll listen myself.\"\n\nSela nodded.\n\nWe spent the afternoon measuring the water marks along her wall. I wrote down the dates she remembered. Aang mapped the winds that carried heavy rain across the valley. We found that the new cart path had been laid on top of a narrow drainage cut, and that its packed gravel had blocked water moving toward the old service passage.\n\nSela fetched a small record book from her kitchen. She had been writing down each time rainwater reached the lowest garden bed. The entries weren't elegant, but they had dates, rough depths and the names of the crops that had been damaged.\n\n\"That one's when the new cart path was finished,\" she said. \"The water started coming through the wrong corner two storms later.\"\n\nAang studied the pages. \"Would you be willing to bring these to the survey committee?\"\n\n\"If they'll copy them instead of taking my only book.\"\n\n\"Of course,\" he began, then caught himself. \"I'll ask them to make a copy while you're there.\"\n\nI added her measurements to our map with a different symbol from the marks we'd made from above. Her records showed what happened on the ground. Our view from Appa had shown the shape of the channel but could not tell us what time of day the water entered her house.\n\nA young gardener from the next plot arrived to say that the suggested alternate path ran across their seed trays. Nobody had told them it was under consideration. Aang apologized, and we crossed the proposed shortcut out of the map instead of arguing it was temporary enough not to count.\n\nBy the time we left, we had three sets of evidence and fewer easy answers. Toph would have enjoyed that, I thought. She liked a problem better when somebody had stopped pretending their own view was complete.\n\n\"Do you think the committee will listen?\" Sela asked.\n\n\"I don't know,\" I admitted. \"But we'll bring your book back, and we'll tell you what they decide.\"\n\nShe looked at Aang.\n\n\"That, at least, is a promise you can make,\" he said.\n\nThe exchange changed nothing about the closed historic barrier. It did change who was allowed to speak before anyone moved more water across another person's home.\n\nThe seventh scratch beneath the well returned to my thoughts.\n\nWe were not ready to open anything. But we had enough evidence to stop treating the flood as a mysterious tantrum by the weather.\n\nBy the time Appa carried us home, the sky was pink.\n\nAang leaned beside me on the saddle, quiet.\n\n\"Do you miss her?\" he asked.\n\n\"Every day.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe looked toward the South, though there was no way to see it from here.\n\n\"Sometimes when I'm away, I worry I'm the one being left behind.\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"By Katara?\"\n\n\"By all of us. The work keeps changing. The world keeps changing. I come home and I'm afraid the shape of our family will be different.\"\n\n\"It will be different.\"\n\nHe looked startled.\n\n\"That's supposed to comfort me?\"\n\n\"Maybe not. But we're allowed to grow. The agreements we make aren't a spell keeping us frozen. We have to keep choosing them.\"\n\nAang considered that.\n\n\"What if we choose differently someday?\"\n\n\"Then we tell the truth. We don't trap people with a promise they made before they knew what they needed.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a while.\n\nThen he leaned close, brushing his forehead against mine.\n\n\"You're impossible.\"\n\n\"Toph says that.\"\n\n\"Toph says everyone is impossible.\"\n\n\"She has a point.\"\n\nHe kissed me gently, then grinned.\n\n\"Appa is watching.\"\n\n\"Appa sneezed on my map. He forfeited the right to judge.\"\n\nBefore we turned toward the landing, Aang made Appa circle once over the Riverstone gardens.\n\n“See the difference in the water?” he asked.\n\nI followed his pointing finger. The new cart path shone pale against the dark ground. Beside it, rainwater had gathered where the field beds stepped down toward the river.\n\n“I can see the puddle,” I said. “I can't see how deep it is.”\n\n“Neither can I.”\n\nHe said it with no embarrassment. The Avatar had crossed countries on the back of a sky bison and could bend wind into impossible shapes, but a map from above did not tell him what it cost a grower to lose seedlings.\n\nWe landed near Sela's garden rather than in it. She came out carrying a tray of bean plants and looked pointedly at Appa's enormous feet.\n\n“Don't park him on the beds,” she said.\n\n“He wouldn't,” Aang began.\n\nAppa sneezed.\n\n“I withdraw that statement,” Aang said.\n\nSela studied the map. “Your route would send runoff behind the clinic.”\n\n“I didn't mark a route,” I said. “Only a possible channel.”\n\n“Then write *possible* in large letters.” She placed her tray on the wall. “Every map somebody brings down from the sky looks finished to people who don't have to live underneath it.”\n\nThe remark stung because it was fair. Aang took the brush from me and added a question mark beside the line. Sela asked us to bring Katara's drainage notes when she returned from the South and to invite Toph before anyone moved a stone.\n\n“We can do that,” Aang said.\n\n“And ask the clinic,” she added.\n\nHe smiled. “And ask the clinic.”\n\nWhen we lifted off again, I held the map away from Appa's muzzle. The ink was still damp. I wanted to believe we had made progress because the page had grown crowded. What we had made was a list of people whose answers mattered. It felt less impressive and more honest.\n\n\nWhen we landed, Toph was waiting with a bowl of dumplings and a list of questions.\n\n\"Well?\" she demanded.\n\n\"We found the old spillway.\"\n\n\"Congratulations. Did the dirt look good from up there?\"\n\n\"Very picturesque,\" I said.\n\n\"Did it tell you what happens underneath the path?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nShe grinned.\n\n\"Then tomorrow we start with the ground.\"\n\nWe spread the stained map across the table. It smelled faintly of bison and evening air. Aang wrote Sela's name beside the garden, with a note that she needed to be present before anyone proposed moving it.\n\nKatara's empty chair stood across from us.\n\nI found myself smiling.\n\nThe survey had not fixed the floodplain. But we had stopped pretending the sky gave us every answer.","word_count":1824}},"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-39":{"number":39,"title":"The Keeper's Refusal","content":"Neri told us the truth without looking at either of us.\n\nThey sat in the archive's west room, where the burned ledger rested under protective cloth. The door was closed, but not locked. Severus had offered to stay and had accepted Neri's request that he wait outside.\n\nIt was the first time Neri had asked me to hear something without calling it a meeting.\n\nMaui sat beside me. He'd come because I wanted company, not because the old keepers belonged to his legend.\n\nNeri folded their hands.\n\n\"I watched someone turn a traveler away during the Closed Bells,\" they said.\n\nThe room seemed to tighten.\n\n\"How old were you?\" I asked.\n\n\"Old enough to understand the words. Young enough to believe an official would know better than I did.\"\n\nThey looked at the ledger.\n\n\"The keeper said the channels were unstable. He said opening the door might endanger everyone. A woman wanted to leave for her homeworld. She had family waiting. She begged him to let her pass.\"\n\n\"Did he?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI heard the sea outside, impossibly distant from Moonroot Archive.\n\n\"What happened to her?\" I asked.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nNeri's voice broke on the last word.\n\nThey took a breath.\n\n\"The keeper ordered the arch sealed. He used the language of safety and protection. He spoke as if refusing her was an act of compassion. I stood there and did nothing.\"\n\nMaui's hand tightened on his knee.\n\nI wanted to say that Neri had been a child, that they hadn't been responsible, that remembering was enough. But the woman had still been turned away. Her family had still waited on the other side.\n\n\"I don't know what you could have done then,\" I said. \"I do know what you can do now.\"\n\nNeri looked up.\n\n\"Tell the truth in the record,\" I said. \"Help us change the rules that allowed it. And don't ask the people affected to make you feel forgiven.\"\n\nThey looked as though I had struck them.\n\n\"I wasn't going to—\"\n\n\"I know,\" I said softly. \"But I think you've been carrying this alone because you wanted a version where your silence made sense.\"\n\nThey stared at the table.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe word was almost inaudible.\n\nWe stayed a while. I asked what Neri remembered about the keeper's instructions, the weather, and the public notice. I did not ask for a name Neri had already said they couldn't reliably provide. Their memory could be a witness to an action without being proof of the scratched signature in the ledger.\n\nMaui listened.\n\nAt one point Neri said, \"I thought he was trying to keep us safe.\"\n\nMaui shifted in his chair.\n\n\"I've told people I was protecting them,\" he said. \"Sometimes I was. Sometimes I was just frightened of being blamed if something went wrong.\"\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"What did you do?\"\n\n\"Things I was wrong about. Spectacularly wrong. I still tried to give them a good ending.\"\n\nHe didn't turn the answer into a story about his greatness.\n\nHe closed one enormous hand around his hook.\n\n\"I hate this kind of room,\" he said. \"Usually the villain is standing right there when I make my entrance. Excellent entrance, by the way. Very memorable.\"\n\nNeri looked at him. \"What would you do if you found the keeper?\"\n\n\"Ask why he locked somebody in.\"\n\n\"And if he said he was protecting everyone?\"\n\nMaui shifted in the chair. He had faced seas taller than the square's rooftops without blinking. This question bothered him more.\n\n\"Want proof. Probably want to shout.\"\n\n\"That isn't a plan.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"It's the first five seconds of a pretty bad one.\"\n\nThey nearly smiled.\n\nOutside, the tide still had to be measured. The sealed lower passage could not be opened by the great Maui declaring himself useful. Neri had not asked for a rescue, only for a witness to listen.\n\n\"I'm excellent at making impossible things sound easy,\" Maui said. \"You don't owe anybody a more exciting version of what happened. And if they insist, send them to me.\"\n\n\"To frighten them?\" Neri asked.\n\n\"To bore them with a six-hour story about myself. Much crueler.\"\n\nNeri let out a breath that was almost a laugh. The ledger remained on the table, unchanged.\n\nLater, Severus returned and asked whether Neri wanted their account documented as a signed statement, an anonymous summary, or kept private while they considered it. They chose a signed statement with restricted access until the wording could be reviewed.\n\nOrin prepared the form.\n\nI stood beside Neri while they read it.\n\nThe final paragraph did not identify the keeper by name. It recorded what Neri had witnessed: a traveler requesting departure, an official refusing it, and a claimed safety reason that had not been documented with an alternative plan.\n\nNeri signed.\n\nThen they put down the pen and wept.\n\nI did not know whether to embrace them. I asked.\n\nThey nodded.\n\nI held them for a moment, feeling how thin their shoulders were beneath the old coat. My own throat hurt. I wanted the world to be the kind of place where admitting wrong made the consequence disappear.\n\nIt wasn't.\n\nTwo days passed before Neri asked Orin for a second copy of the testimony.\n\nWe were in Moonroot's sorting room, where scraps of damaged paper waited in shallow trays. Orin had labeled each tray by the document it came from, not by the conclusion somebody hoped it would support.\n\n\"I've been reading what I signed,\" Neri said. \"I didn't remember whether the traveler was alone.\"\n\nOrin stopped writing. \"Then we can amend the statement.\"\n\n\"Would that make the whole thing unreliable?\"\n\n\"It would make the amendment visible,\" Orin said. \"That's different.\"\n\nNeri sat down. I could see their hands shaking around the edge of the chair. Maui brought a cup of water and set it nearby without making them take it.\n\n\"I kept seeing the child's red sleeve,\" Neri said. \"The traveler might have been holding their hand. Or the child might have stood beside the official. I can't make the memory settle.\"\n\nNobody offered to tell them which version sounded more likely.\n\nOrin recorded the uncertainty in an addendum and preserved the original wording alongside it. Neri could still choose whether the statement was read publicly. The restricted copy would keep both versions, with the date of the correction and the reason for it.\n\n\"I thought remembering would make me feel less frightened,\" Neri admitted.\n\n\"Sometimes remembering gives you more than you know how to use,\" I said.\n\nMaui shifted on the other side of the table. \"I hate this part,\" he said. \"No monster to punch. No glorious entrance. Just the thing you did, staring at you until you stop trying to make it sound better.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb along the curve of his hook before speaking again.\n\n\"I could talk an island into calling a mistake a victory,\" he said, a crooked grin appearing. \"Doesn't change what happened. Moana was *very* good at reminding me of that. Usually while I was in the middle of my best speech.\"\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"You can tell those parts?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\" His grin was quick and painful. \"Depends who's listening. The tattoos are brutal critics.\"\n\nMini Maui popped up and pointed at a dark little image farther across his chest, one Maui rarely called attention to. He covered it with his hand. I wasn't going to ask him to turn his history into entertainment for this meeting.\n\n\"The missing line matters,\" he told Neri. \"I'm good at making stories sound wonderful. Doesn't make every part true.\"\n\nIt was more honest than one of his polished lessons. His pride hadn't disappeared. Neither had his guilt. He could still be a storyteller without making the archive's evidence follow his preferred ending.\n\nNeri looked at him.\n\n\"Did you?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. And sometimes I only learned I should have listened earlier.\"\n\nThe answer made Neri breathe a little more easily.\n\nBefore we left, Orin showed us where the public copy would omit the child's details. The testimony was about a forced refusal of passage. Nobody needed to turn a half-remembered child into a clue for strangers to hunt.\n\nOn the walk to the square, Maui reached for my hand. I accepted, even though I was still thinking about the scarred page and Neri's face when they said they weren't sure.\n\n\"You're allowed not to know everything,\" he said.\n\n\"So are you.\"\n\n\"Terrible rule. It spoils all my dramatic speeches.\"\n\n\"Maybe one or two of them will survive.\"\n\nHe grinned. It felt good to laugh without making the day feel smaller.\n\nAt the next council session, Neri gave permission to read the verified portion of their testimony. The room listened. A woman in the audience asked whether other witnesses could be contacted without being pressured to relive what had happened.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said. \"And they may refuse.\"\n\nSomeone asked whether the old keeper had meant well.\n\nI could feel the room searching for an easier ending.\n\nI stood.\n\n\"Intention matters to understanding how decisions were made,\" I said. \"It doesn't make a locked exit harmless. A person who wasn't allowed to leave was still denied a choice.\"\n\nEdda laid two diagrams on the table before the vote. One showed the old maintenance passage beneath Closed Bells, marked in red. The other showed the traveler Threadways with separate approaches and exits.\n\n“These are not the same doors,” she said.\n\nThe man beside the notice board frowned. “But people used the lower route in the flood.”\n\n“People attempted to,” Neri answered. “That is part of what my statement describes. It does not make the passage safe today.”\n\nMaui leaned forward. “Then we fix it. I can lift the stone—”\n\n“Not while people are beneath it,” Edda said.\n\nHis mouth stayed open for half a second. Mini Maui put both hands over its face.\n\n“Right,” Maui said. “Okay. No surprise demolition. Put that in the record.”\n\nI could almost hear his pride creak under the weight of choosing not to be impressive.\n\nA woman from the square asked whether Neri's uncertain memory of the child's sleeve made the entire testimony worthless.\n\n“No,” Orin said. “We have a dated record of the amendment. The uncertainty is part of the evidence. Pretending otherwise would make it less trustworthy.”\n\nThe woman considered that. She wasn't convinced, but she lowered her hand and let the next speaker be heard. Fen wanted to know who would monitor the tide before any surface trial. Lio wanted to know whether the skiff crews would be paid for the extra crossings. Mara wanted the public notices written plainly enough for somebody arriving frightened and wet.\n\nI looked at the two diagrams. The point was not to replace one heroic keeper with another. It was to build a system in which a frightened traveler could ask what was safe, hear an honest answer and still have choices.\n\nMaui slid his hook farther away from the table.\n\n“I'm available for moving stones,” he said. “After the inspectors tell me which stones. And after the people nearby agree.”\n\nNeri laughed once, surprised enough that I turned toward them. It was not forgiveness. It was a small sound in a room that had contained almost none all afternoon.\n\n\nThe council approved a review of every emergency crossing procedure. Volunteers would be trained to explain safe options and record only the information necessary to help. The lower service passage would stay closed until the structural team could inspect it; closure of an unsafe maintenance tunnel would never be used to justify blocking the independent traveler Threadways.\n\nThat distinction took an entire afternoon to write correctly.\n\nAfterward, Maui and I walked to the cove.\n\n\"I wanted to shout,\" he admitted.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You didn't.\"\n\n\"I wanted to.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Why didn't you?\"\n\n\"Because Neri wasn't asking me to make them feel good. They were trying to say something difficult. I wanted to hear it without turning their shame into my performance.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\nThe light had grown gold over the sea-glass orchard. I could smell wet earth and the faint spice of rosemary. The little bench stood empty.\n\nWe sat.\n\n\"I was wrong about many things,\" Maui said. \"Still am, probably.\"\n\n\"Very probably.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"Do you think Neri's testimony will help?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Not because it ends the question. Because people can now ask better questions.\"\n\nHe put his hand over mine.\n\nWe watched a boat make the safe turn toward the upper landing, its lantern moving steadily through the evening light.\n\nThe harbor did not become innocent because somebody had spoken.\n\nBut the words were out in the open.\n\nThat mattered.","word_count":2119}}};for(const s of library.series){const byId=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=byId[ch.id];if(!e||e.number!==ch.chapter_number)continue;ch.content=e.content;ch.title=e.title;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}})();
